# GitHub Copilot in VS Code → OTLP collector.
#
# These are NOT environment variables. VS Code Copilot is configured through
# settings.json; the file is kept here so all three surfaces sit together.
#
# Verified 2026-09-21 by reading the shipping build directly. Copilot is a
# BUILT-IN extension of VS Code, not a marketplace install, so the whole surface
# is on disk with no seat and no sign-in — under the VS Code application bundle,
# Contents/Resources/app, in the extensions directory.
#
# Pinned to copilot-chat 0.60.0, host VS Code 1.132.0. `just copilot-check`
# re-verifies every key below against whatever you have installed.
#
# VS Code runs TWO telemetry producers: the Copilot Chat extension, and an agent
# host process that runs the CLI runtime. The enterprise docs confirm managed
# telemetry "applies to both the Copilot Chat extension and the agent host
# process".
#
# RETRACTED: an earlier revision of this file claimed a `chat.agentHost.otel.*`
# settings namespace exists. It does not — zero occurrences in the agent host,
# the extension bundle or package.json. The claim came from a grep that matched
# `[agentHost-otel]` log tags. There is no agent-host settings namespace; the
# eleven github.copilot.chat.otel.* keys are the whole surface.
#
# What the agent host actually does: binds an OTLP receiver on an ephemeral
# 127.0.0.1 port, spawns the CLI as a stdio child pointed at it, and relays.
# It is a relay, not a second exporter, so it follows the settings below.
#
# Settings landed in VS Code 1.119 (2026-05-06).
#   https://code.visualstudio.com/updates/v1_119
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# settings.json — all eleven keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.enabled": true,
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.exporterType": "otlp-http",
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.protocol": "",
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.otlpEndpoint": "http://localhost:4318",
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.captureContent": false,
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.headers": {},
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.serviceName": "",
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.resourceAttributes": {},
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.maxAttributeSizeChars": 0,
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.outfile": "",
#   "github.copilot.chat.otel.dbSpanExporter.enabled": false
#
# exporterType accepts otlp-http, otlp-grpc, console, file. Note that otlp-grpc
# applies to the extension only — the CLI runtime uses HTTP regardless.
#
# protocol accepts "", http/json, http/protobuf, grpc. Empty means http/json, so
# the extension's default wire format is JSON, not protobuf.
#
# Unlike the CLI, the extension exports to http://localhost:4318 perfectly well.
# That asymmetry is why a single endpoint appears to work and silently collects
# only half your data. See copilot-cli.env, next to this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three keys that do more than they look like they do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# outfile — this, NOT exporterType, is what selects the file exporter. The
# branch needs exporterType=="file" AND a non-empty outfile, so exporterType on
# its own writes no file and falls through to OTLP on localhost:4318. A
# non-empty outfile meanwhile forces the file exporter whatever exporterType
# says. It is also a trap: the file exporter writes the literal string {} for
# every span. Read README trap 8 before using it.
#
# dbSpanExporter.enabled — the only span sink that runs in parallel with OTLP,
# and spans only. On its own it does the OPPOSITE of what it looks like: with
# OTel not explicitly enabled, the primary span exporter becomes a null exporter
# that drops every batch and reports success, and logs and metrics go to the
# console. Always pair it with enabled=true.
#
# maxAttributeSizeChars — 0, the default, DISABLES truncation, so a backend with
# a per-attribute size cap receives payloads it may reject. Set a positive value
# to match your backend. Truncated values are suffixed
# "...[truncated, original N chars]".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth headers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `github.copilot.chat.otel.headers` is a normal user setting: an object of
# { "key": "value" } applied directly to the OTLP exporter. It merges per key
# with OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS, and the environment wins on a collision.
#
# That matters on macOS. A GUI-launched VS Code inherits launchd's minimal
# environment rather than your shell's, so an exported OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS
# is invisible unless you launch from a terminal. The setting is read either way.
#
# The cost: settings.json is plaintext and frequently tracked in a dotfiles repo.
# `just leaks` scans *.env and does NOT scan settings.json. Use a token scoped to
# ingest only.
#
# Precedence: enterprise policy → environment variable → user setting → default.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All eleven keys are scope: application
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Workspace and folder settings CANNOT set them. That is the structural fix for
# the pre-1.123.1 defect where a cloned repo could flip otel.enabled,
# captureContent or otlpEndpoint on you.
#
# Five are further marked "user settings only": serviceName, resourceAttributes,
# headers, maxAttributeSizeChars and dbSpanExporter.enabled. That phrase means
# no environment override of their own — it does NOT mean policy cannot reach
# them. The first three still resolve a policy value, and policy wins. Only
# maxAttributeSizeChars and dbSpanExporter.enabled have no policy path.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Enterprise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Enterprise-managed export shipped 2026-07-08 and applies to both the extension
# and the agent host. A managed value ALWAYS wins over environment variables and
# user settings. Scope is enterprise, not organisation. Keys live under a
# `telemetry` block: enabled, endpoint, protocol, captureContent,
# lockCaptureContent, serviceName, resourceAttributes, headers.
#
# Managed protocol accepts http/json or http/protobuf only — a third, narrower
# value set than either of the two above.

# Intentionally empty. Every knob on this surface is a settings.json key. The
# OTEL_* variables still work and still outrank the settings, but since 0.60.0
# none of them is required — not even for auth headers.
